// Solution — Module 06, Exercise 02: strlen Without A Terminator
//
// Task 1: strlen(good) is 2. The block is three bytes; the string is two
// characters. The terminator is stored but never counted, which is why
// "how many bytes do I need" and "how long is the string" are different
// questions with different answers, forever.
//
// Task 2: uncommenting strlen(bad) gives
//
//   area does not exist at line 18, column 11
//     18 |     while p[n] != 0 {
//   byte 2..2 is outside this pine's block, which covers 0..1
//   Stack trace:
//     strlen()
//
// The fault lands on the loop's own condition — the read that tests for the
// terminator is the read that goes out of bounds. Note the stack trace names
// strlen(), not the caller. In C the fault would also happen inside strlen,
// which is a large part of why these bugs are hard to attribute: the function
// that crashes is not the function that made the mistake.
//
// Task 3: byte 2. The block covers 0..1, so 2 is the first index past the
// end. strlen read bytes 0 and 1, found 72 and 73, neither of them zero, and
// asked for byte 2 — because that is all it can do. It has no size to check
// against.
//
// Task 4: in real C, nothing stops it. strlen keeps reading past the block
// into the heap: allocator metadata, other allocations, freed memory. It
// returns when it happens to encounter a zero byte, so the answer is
// whatever the neighbouring memory contains — 2, or 40, or 4000, and a
// different number on the next run. What makes it stop is either a stray
// zero or an unmapped page, and only the second one is a crash you would
// notice. This is why an unterminated string is not a "short string" bug; it
// is an unbounded read of memory you do not own.

fn strlen(p) {
    n = 0
    while p[n] != 0 {
        n = n + 1
    }
    return n
}

// Task 1: a properly terminated string.
good = garrison(3)
good[0] = 72
good[1] = 73
good[2] = 0

bathysphere(good)
print(f"strlen = {strlen(good)}")
print(f"block is 3 bytes, string is {strlen(good)} characters")

// The terminator is what makes length findable. Move it and the length
// changes without a single letter being touched.
print("")
good[1] = 0
bathysphere(good)
print(f"same block, terminator moved to index 1 — strlen is now {strlen(good)}")

// BONUS: `while (i < strlen(s))` re-walks the whole string on every
// iteration, so a loop over an N-character string does N passes of N steps.
// That is O(N^2) to do something that should be O(N), and it is invisible in
// the source — the loop reads like any other. Hoist the strlen out.
print("")
print("strlen is a walk, not a lookup: calling it in a loop condition")
print("re-walks the string every single iteration.")

scorch(good)

// Task 2 and 3: the unterminated block. Ends the program, so it goes last.
bad = garrison(2)
bad[0] = 72
bad[1] = 73

print("")
bathysphere(bad)
print("two letters, no terminator - now walking it with strlen...")
print(f"unreachable: {strlen(bad)}")
